Antiqua&quot;,serif;mso-fareast-font-family:&#xA;&quot;Book Antiqua&quot;;mso-bidi-font-family:&quot;Book Antiqua&quot;;color:black\"><span style=\"mso-list:Ignore\">[12]<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">&nbsp; </span></span></span><span lang=\"EN-IN\" style=\"font-size:9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Book Antiqua&quot;,serif;mso-bidi-font-family:&#xA;&quot;Times New Roman&quot;;color:black;background:white\">Minot,<span style=\"mso-spacerun:yes\">&nbsp; </span>N.<span style=\"mso-spacerun:yes\">&nbsp; </span>and<span style=\"mso-spacerun:yes\">&nbsp; </span>L.<span style=\"mso-spacerun:yes\">&nbsp; </span>Daniels.<span style=\"mso-spacerun:yes\">&nbsp; </span>2005.<span style=\"mso-spacerun:yes\">&nbsp; </span>&ldquo;Impact<span style=\"mso-spacerun:yes\">&nbsp; </span>of declining global cotton prices on rural poverty in Benin&rdquo;. Agricultural Economics, 33: 453-466.</span><span lang=\"EN-IN\" style=\"font-size:9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif;color:black;&#xA;background:white\">â€</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListParagraphCxSpLast\" style=\"margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;&#xA;margin-bottom:0in;margin-left:.25in;mso-add-space:auto;text-align:justify;&#xA;text-indent:-.25in;line-height:normal;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo1\"><span lang=\"EN-IN\" style=\"font-size:9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Book Antiqua&quot;,serif;mso-fareast-font-family:&#xA;&quot;Book Antiqua&quot;;mso-bidi-font-family:&quot;Book Antiqua&quot;;color:black\"><span style=\"mso-list:Ignore\">[13]<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">&nbsp; </span></span></span><span lang=\"EN-IN\" style=\"font-size:9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Book Antiqua&quot;,serif;mso-bidi-font-family:&#xA;&quot;Times New Roman&quot;;color:black;background:white\">under different<span style=\"mso-spacerun:yes\">&nbsp; </span>socio-economic environments in Ethiopia&rdquo;. Agricultural Economics, 30:215-228. Vol. 39,</span></p>', 'Modern Technology Gross Domestic Product Pesticides, Crop Sensors, Biotechnology And Inorganic Fertilizers..', 'Porika, R. (2024). Modern Agricultural Technology, Its Importance, Usage for the Improvement of Agriculture. In The American Journal of Science and Medical Research (http://ajsmrjournal.com/issueslist.php?cat_id=39, Vol. 10, Number 1, pp. 1â€“4). https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11005183', 'Received: 25 November 2023;  Accepted: 11 January 2024; Published: 18 January, 2024', 1);
INSERT INTO `ajsmr_abstracts` (`contentid`, `conttitle`, `issuedetails`, `authors`, `journal`, `doi`, `abstract`, `abstractsdesc`, `referencestxt`, `keywords`, `howtoguide`, `articledates`, `status`) VALUES
(115, 'Examination of Telangana Governments Policies Aligned with SDGs', 'Research Article I Volume 10 I Issue 1 I 2024', 'Jannu Vijaya kumar, Raju Porika, Srinivas Devanaveni', 'The American Journal of Science and Medical Research, Volume 10, Issue 1, 2024, pp 5-7', 'https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11044133', '../pdffiles/cimg015253_10.1.2.kdcraju5-7.pdf', 'This study discuss various government programs in Telangana, highlighting their strengths and weaknesses. The Rythu Bandhu program is noted as being more expensive due to lack of limitations, leading to potential misuse. Similarly, the Rythu Bhima program is praised but criticized for not being enforced at specific times. Asara pensions are commended, but the misuse by ineligible candidates is a concern. The Kalyana Lakshmi and Shaadi Mubarak programs are seen as beneficial but susceptible to misuse. The Dalita Bandhu program receives mixed feedback, with beneficiaries satisfied but non-beneficiaries opposed. BC Bandhu is criticized for not reaching grassroots levels effectively. Mission Kakatiya and Mission Bhagiratha are recognized as good programs, but concerns about fund misuse and infrastructure issues are raised. The Medigadda lift irrigation project is noted for its high cost and recent infrastructure problems. Other programs such as Abhaya Hastam, Amma Vodi, Arogyasri, Amrutha Hastham, free bus passes, double bedroom program, free electricity supply, and LPG cylinder subsidies are also discussed in terms of their implementation challenges and potential negative impacts. The abstract provides a comprehensive overview of the various government programs in Telangana, highlighting both their positive aspects and areas for improvement.', '<p><span><span>[1]<span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span>TRS Manifesto</span></p>\r\n<p><span><span>[2]<span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span>Congress Manifesto</span></p>\r\n<p><span><span>[3]<span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span>Telangana Socio Economic Outlook 2023 &ndash; Planning department, Government of Telangana</span></p>\r\n<p><span><span>[4]<span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span>Telangana Statistical Abstract</span></p>\r\n<p><span><span>[5]<span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span>Public policy in India Maniram Sharma www.mahaveerpublications.com 21 January 2023</span></p>\r\n<p><span><span>[6]<span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span>Public policy and politics in India oxford India paperbacks Kuldeep Mathur</span></p>\r\n<p><span><span>[7]<span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span>Public policy formulation, implementation and Evaluation, R .K. Sapru and Y.Sapru 1 January 2019</span></p>', 'SDGs, SRC, Rythu Bandhu, Rythu Bheema, Asara pensions.', 'Porika, R. (2024). Examination of Telangana Governments Policies Aligned with SDGs. In The American Journal of Science and Medical Research, 2024 (Vol. 10, Number 1, pp. 5â€“7). https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11044133', 'Received: 28 November 2023;  Accepted: 18 January 2024; Published: 20 January, 2024', 1),
(116, 'Studies on Biologically Active Compounds Produced by Certain Members of Non Heterocystous Cyanobacteria', 'Original Research Article I Volume 10 I Issue 2 I 2024', 'Rajitha Tungani and Raju Porika', 'The American Journal of Science and Medical Research (2024), 10(2); 1-6', 'https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11054412', '../pdffiles/cimg015213_10.2.1kdcraju1-6.pdf', 'Cyanobacteria, also called blue-green algae, are O2-evolving photosynthetic prokaryotes, many (though not all) of which can fix N2. N2-fixing (diazotrophic) cyanobacteria can be broadly grouped as heterocystous and non-heterocystous forms. All heterocystous forms are capable of aerobic N2 fixation and, in the absence of combined nitrogen, 5â€“10% of their vegetative cells differentiate into specialized cells called heterocysts that provide an environment suitable for the functioning of nitrogenase (the enzyme that catalyses N2 fixation). Unlike vegetative cells, heterocysts are photo synthetically inactive. They do not fix CO2, nor do they produce O2. Cyanobacteria generally remain potential source for investigations as prospective and excellent sources of biologically active constituents produced during primary and especially secondary metabolism. The soil samples containing NHC were collected from different paddy fields in and around Warangal district, Telangana State. About 10 samples were collected from different locations and utilized for enrichment studies in BGâ€“11 medium without nitrogen supplementation. Strains were transferred in liquid nutrient BG 11 medium all the collected Non Heterocystous cyano bacteria strains were maintained at 7.2 pH, 28+20C and with a 14/10-hour light/dark cycle photoperiod. The morphology of Oscillatoria curviceps, Oscillatoria princeps and Oscillatoria acuta was influenced similarly by physicochemical properties of soil. Although Oscillatoria corntiana and Oscillatoria quadrapanculata belong to the same order and family, the latter was influenced differently due to the physicochemical properties of the soil. All extracts exhibited different degrees of antimicrobial activity unrelatedly to the tested pathogen. ', '<p>&nbsp;<span style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -22.5pt; font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Book Antiqua&quot;, serif;\">[1]<span style=\"font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 7pt; line-height: normal; font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;;\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</span></span><b style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -22.5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:9.0pt;font-family:&#10;&quot;Book Antiqua&quot;,serif;mso-bidi-font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;;mso-ansi-language:&#10;EN-US\">Abdel-Aziz, N.A.M. and Abdel-Raouf, N. 2002</span></b><span style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -22.5pt; font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Book Antiqua&quot;, serif;\">. Algal metabolites as alternative acaricide against Tetranychus utrica Koch (Acari: Tetranychidae).International Conference for Development and the Environment in the Arab world, March, 26-28,Assuit University Centre for Environmental Studies,Assuit Univ., Assuit, Egypt.pp 43-51.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst\" style=\"margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;&#10;margin-bottom:0in;margin-left:22.5pt;mso-add-space:auto;text-align:justify;&#10;text-indent:-22.5pt;line-height:normal;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo1\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle\" style=\"margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;&#10;margin-bottom:0in;margin-left:22.5pt;mso-add-space:auto;text-align:justify;&#10;text-indent:-22.5pt;line-height:normal;mso-list:l0 level1 lfo1\"><!--[if !supportLists]--><span style=\"font-size:9.0pt;font-family:&quot;Book Antiqua&quot;,serif;mso-fareast-font-family:&#10;&quot;Book Antiqua&quot;;mso-bidi-font-